# Git Commit Message Instructions

You must generate commit messages following the **Conventional Commits** format and the specific organizational structure below.

## 1. Structure
- **Format**: `<type>: <description>`
- **Body**: Include a blank line after the title, followed by the issue URL.

## 2. Allowed Types
| Type | Description |
| :--- | :--- |
| **feat** | A new feature |
| **fix** | A bug fix |
| **chore** | Routine maintenance or tasks not affecting users |
| **docs** | Documentation changes |
| **style** | Formatting, semicolons, etc. (no logic change) |
| **refactor** | Code changes (no bug fix or new feature) |
| **perf** | Performance improvements |
| **test** | Adding or updating tests |
| **build** | Build system or external dependencies |
| **ci** | CI/CD configuration and scripts |
| **revert** | Reverts a previous commit |
| **deps** | Dependency updates |
| **security** | Security-related fixes or improvements |
| **infra** | Infrastructure-related changes |

## 3. Formatting Rules
- **Mood**: Use the **imperative** mood (e.g., "Add feature" instead of "Added feature").
- **Case**: The description should start with a capital letter.
- **Length**: Keep the title line under 50 characters.
- **Context**: If an issue link is available in the staged changes or branch name, include it.

## 4. Examples
- `fix: Adjust weekly report links to avoid incorrect headers`
- `feat: Add SQL validation`
